            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-xl",
            "max_width_desktop": "135ch",
            "richtext": "<p>If you're thinking about running an air purifier 24/7 for continuous protection, which is what makes them effective for keeping allergens and particulates down, the electricity question matters. You're looking at potentially hundreds of dollars over time, so being smart about it makes sense. </p><p>Let us show you which models use the least electricity and how this technology actually works.</p><h2>Energy Efficient Air Purifier Technology</h2><p>Most traditional air purifiers use dense mechanical filters that require high fan speeds to push air through, which means more energy consumption. Blueair's approach is different with their HEPASilent™ technology, which combines both electrostatic and mechanical filtration. According to Blueair, this combination allows them to use less dense filters, which means:</p><ul><li>The fan doesn't have to work as hard.</li><li>Air moves through more efficiently.</li><li>Energy consumption stays down.</li><li>CADR (the amount of clean air delivered) remains high</li><li>Noise levels stay lower.</li></ul><p>Blueair notes that their devices are developed with low power consumption in mind, ensuring that both environmental impact and cost of ownership are kept as low as possible. Many of their models carry<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/pages/blueair-performance\"> Energy Star certification</a>, a globally recognized program by the EPA and U.S. Department of Energy.</p><h2>Low-Watt Air Purifiers: Blueair Model Energy Consumption</h2>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "s3"
          }
        },
        "image_caption_KnqYBR": {
          "type": "image_caption",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-0",
            "padding_top_mobile": "pt-0",
