            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-lg",
            "max_width_desktop": "135ch",
            "richtext": "<p>Here's what matters for 24/7 operation: even small differences in wattage add up over the year. If a purifier runs continuously, that's 8,760 hours annually.</p><p>Blue Pure 511i Max: Most Energy Efficient</p><p>Uses 2.5-20W depending on fan speed. This is the<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/products/blue-pure-511i-max\"> most compact model</a> in Blueair's lineup, making it ideal for bedrooms or personal offices. At maximum, that's about 175 kWh annually. Many people run this on lower or medium fan speeds 24/7, which keeps actual consumption significantly lower. According to<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/blogs/news/the-complete-guide-to-selecting-an-air-purifier-based-on-room-size\"> Blueair's guidance on room selection</a>, this model covers 180-189 sq ft effectively.</p><p>Blue Pure 311i Max: Mid-Range Efficiency</p><p>Uses 10-38W. This model covers a<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/products/blue-pure-311i-max\"> larger space</a> than the 411i, making it suitable for living rooms while staying reasonably efficient.</p><p>Blue Pure 211i Max: Larger Coverage Area</p><p>Uses 46W at maximum. This is Blueair's<a href=\"https://www.blueair.com/products/blue-pure-211i-max\"> most powerful option for extra-large rooms</a>, covering significantly more space while still maintaining efficiency through the HEPASilent™ approach.</p><p>For comparison, traditional mechanical-only HEPA purifiers often require 75-150W continuously, which is substantially higher than Blueair's range.</p><h2>Low Energy Air Purifier: Real-World Usage Scenarios</h2>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
        },
        "image_caption_qy8nHW": {
          "type": "image_caption",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-0",
